              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.23"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.438,
            0.704,
            0.462
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Making Parallel Lines through Paper Folding"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.47,
            0.847,
            0.526
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Let us try to do the same with paper folding. For a line l (given as a crease), how do we make a line parallel to l such that it passes through point A?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.525,
            0.847,
            0.582
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "We know how to fold a piece of paper to get a line perpendicular to l. Now, try to fold a perpendicular to l such that it passes through point A. Let us call this new crease t."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.581,
            0.847,
            0.619
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Now, fold a line perpendicular to t passing through A again. Let us call this line m. The lines l and m are parallel to each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
